"Before there was paper, people in China wrote on hard things. More than 3,000 years ago, in the late Shang dynasty, people carved characters on tortoise shells and animal bones, mostly the shoulder blades of cattle. We call this writing oracle bone script. Taiwan's Ministry of Education calls it the earliest Chinese writing that survives on real objects.": "f423903dccd2",
"Before there was paper, people in China wrote on hard things. More than 3,000 years ago, in the late Shang dynasty, people carved characters on tortoise shells and animal bones, mostly the shoulder blades of cattle. We call this writing oracle bone script. Taiwan's Ministry of Education calls it the earliest Chinese writing that survives on real objects. Why carve on shells? The Shang kings asked many questions about what would happen. Diviners cut hollows into the back of a shell or bone and pressed the red-hot end of a wooden stick into them. Cracks appeared on the front, shaped a bit like the character 卜, which means to divine. Then they carved the record beside the cracks: the date, the question, and the answer. Nobody today knows exactly how they read the cracks. Many early characters began as pictures of things. 日 was the sun, 月 a crescent moon, 山 three mountain peaks, 水 a stream with drops of water, 人 a person seen from the side, and 馬 a horse standing on its tail, with a big eye and a mane. About 1,900 years ago, the scholar Xu Shen called this way of making characters 象形, drawing the shape of a thing, and he gave 日 and 月 as his examples. Writing was also cast or carved on bronze. In the Shang and Zhou dynasties, words were put on bronze vessels such as the ding, a pot with three legs. These lines are thicker and rounder than carved lines. To study them on paper, people make rubbings: paper is pressed onto the writing and patted with ink, and the sunken characters stay white. The Mao Gong Ding in the National Palace Museum in Taipei holds about 500 characters in 32 lines. In 221 BCE, the state of Qin united China. The Shiji, a famous history, records that the First Emperor made the writing the same everywhere. Li Si and other officials simplified the older script into a standard form called small seal script, with smooth lines of even width. After seal script came clerical script, flat and wide (Lesson 6), and then regular script, the way we write today (Lesson 7). In this lesson, crack and carve a shell in 3D, make a rubbing from a bronze ding, and watch a brush write seal script. Then pull the slider to see one character change over 3,000 years.": "f4d057f11f95",
